---
/**
 * The privacy page for Tortie for iPhone and Tortie for Mac (Tortie Phase
 * 333.5, research 136 §12.5 as revised by research 140 §10.4).
 *
 * EVERY SENTENCE HERE IS A CLAIM ABOUT WHAT LEAVES A PERSON'S PHONE, and each
 * one is mapped to the Tortie source line that makes it true in the phase's
 * re-derivation table. Change a sentence only with a line that proves it.
 * The SpecStory paragraph is the one Mac exception, and it stands while
 * WRAP_FLAGS in Tortie's src/main/specstory/wrap.ts omits
 * --no-usage-analytics (research 37 §3.1).
 * Words this page never uses: "beta", "remote desktop", "mirror", "stream"
 * and "SSH" (his rulings of 2026-10-07).
 *
 * Keep an inline link on the same source line as the word before it: the
 * build collapses a line break before a tag to nothing.
 */
import PlainPage from "../components/PlainPage.astro";
---

<PlainPage
  title="Privacy · Tortie"
  description="What Tortie for iPhone sends, where it goes and what it keeps. Ita Vero, LLC collects none of your data and runs no server the phone's data passes through."
  heading="Privacy"
  lead="What Tortie for iPhone sends, where it goes and what it keeps."
  note="Last updated 7 October 2026"
>
  <section aria-label="Summary">
    <p>
      Tortie for iPhone and Tortie for Mac are made by Ita Vero, LLC. We do not collect your data.
      Tortie for iPhone needs no account with us and has no analytics and no advertising, and no data passes
      through a server we run.
    </p>
    <p>
      On your Mac, SpecStory capture is off until you turn it on. When it is on, Tortie runs SpecStory, which
      sends usage analytics to SpecStory, including the session’s folder and the command that started the
      agent. If you are signed in to SpecStory, it also uploads the conversation to SpecStory Cloud.
    </p>
    <p>
      While Tortie for iPhone is tested through Apple’s TestFlight, Apple sends us its crash reports,
      and any feedback or screenshot you choose to send, under Apple’s terms.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="what-leaves">
    <h2>What leaves your iPhone</h2>
    <p>
      Tortie for iPhone talks only to the Mac you pair it with. To pair, it sends that Mac its public keys
      and a generic name such as “iPhone”, sealed so only your Mac can read them. Then it sends signed
      requests to read your sessions, to end one you choose, and to pass on what you type or tap. Your Mac
      answers with your sessions: their names, projects, status, conversations and terminals.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="terminal">
    <h2>Your terminal</h2>
    <p>
      When you open a session’s terminal, your Mac sends your phone what it shows and what it printed
      before. Every key you type there goes into that session, as if you typed it at your Mac. If the
      session runs a coding agent, that agent may send what you type to the company that makes it, under
      that company’s terms. Your phone keeps none of it: no file, no log and no picture, only text you
      copy yourself.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="connection">
    <h2>How it gets there</h2>
    <p>
      Your phone reaches your Mac over the internet through Tailscale Funnel, which your Mac runs under your
      own Tailscale account. The connection is encrypted from your phone to your Mac. Tailscale’s relay can
      see your phone’s internet address, your Mac’s name, when you connect and how much is sent, but not
      what is sent.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="mac-name">
    <h2>Your Mac’s name</h2>
    <p>
      While your Mac lets your phone reach it, anyone on the internet can look up its Tailscale name.
      That name often holds your first name. You can <a href="/support/#rename">rename your Mac in Tailscale</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="alerts">
    <h2>Alerts</h2>
    <p>
      Only Tortie’s publisher can send alerts for now. Your phone asks iOS for alerts only when its Mac says
      it can send them, and then gives that Mac, sealed, the address Apple uses to reach it. Each alert goes through
      Apple. It can say a session’s name, project, agent, machine and status, and never its question or its
      terminal.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="camera">
    <h2>Camera, Face ID and Touch ID</h2>
    <p>
      Tortie uses the camera only to read the pairing code, and receives the code’s text, never a picture.
      It asks for Face ID, Touch ID or your passcode only before you end a session. iOS checks it and tells
      Tortie only whether you were confirmed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="kept">
    <h2>What your phone keeps</h2>
    <p>
      Your phone keeps its pairing in this iPhone’s Keychain, never synced or restored to another phone. It
      also keeps which sessions the Sessions tab shows, and how it groups and sorts them. Tortie stores
      nothing your Mac sends. If you delete Tortie, the pairing can stay in the Keychain, but a new install
      forgets it before reading anything, and you pair again.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cut-off">
    <h2>Cutting off a phone</h2>
    <p>
      On your Mac, press Remove beside the phone in Settings then Phone, or switch off Let my phone reach
      this Mac. Either cuts it off at once, without the phone. Unpair this iPhone, in the phone’s Settings,
      makes the phone forget your Mac, and your Mac lists it until you press Remove.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="website">
    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      tortie.sh counts page views with Vercel Web Analytics, this page included.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="contact">
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Email <a href="mailto:support@tortie.sh">support@tortie.sh</a>.</p>
    <p class="plain-fine">© 2026 Ita Vero, LLC</p>
  </section>
</PlainPage>
